# Engineering best practices

Layering (controller/service/model) is covered in `flask-service-layering.mdc`.
This rule is about cross-cutting hygiene within a layer, based on patterns
already found duplicated or inconsistently adopted in this codebase — check
these before adding a new instance of the same shape.

## Use `transactional()`, don't hand-roll the same try/except again

`app/core/transaction.py` already provides this, and it is the right shape —
commits on success, rolls back and converts an `IntegrityError` into a clean
`ConflictError`, re-raises anything else for the global handler to turn into a
generic 500:

```python
from app.core.transaction import transactional

with transactional():
    db.session.add(nova_viagem)
    # ... more writes ...
```

It is currently used in 4 places, all in `viagens_service.py`
(`app/services/viagens_service.py:139,290,321,382`). Roughly 35 other mutation
call sites across `user_service.py`, `rotas_service.py`, `pontos_service.py`,
`aluno_service.py`, `onibus_service.py`, `instituicao_service.py`,
`ocorrencia_service.py`, `notificacao_service.py`, and `auth_service.py`
hand-roll a strictly worse version instead:

```python
# Don't write this — it already exists, and it leaks exception text (below)
except Exception as e:
    db.session.rollback()
    logger.error(f"Error doing X: {e}", exc_info=True)
    raise AppError(f"Erro ao fazer X: {str(e)}", 500)
```

When touching a function that still uses the manual form, prefer switching it
to `transactional()` over adding a second copy of the same hand-rolled
try/except.

## Never put exception text in a client-facing message

`app/core/error_handlers.py` returns `AppError.message` **verbatim** in the
JSON response body. The manual pattern above interpolates `str(e)` into that
message — which can contain SQL fragments, constraint names, or driver
internals — and this is inconsistent with the catch-all `Exception` handler
two levels up, which deliberately returns a generic "Erro interno do
servidor" for the same failure class. `transactional()` avoids this
automatically by never wrapping the exception into an `AppError` message in
the first place. If you're not using `transactional()` for some reason, at
minimum:

```python
except Exception as e:
    db.session.rollback()
    logger.error("Error doing X", exc_info=True)   # detail goes to logs
    raise AppError("Erro ao fazer X", 500)            # generic message to client
```

## Don't duplicate a lookup-or-404 across services

The "get by id or raise `NotFoundError`" shape for `User` is duplicated 10+
times across `app/services/*.py` (`pontos_service`, `onibus_service`,
`rotas_service`, `instituicao_service`, `viagens_service`, ...), while
`user_service.py` already has a private `_get_user_or_404` doing the same
thing for its own file only. Before writing another
`x = Model.query.get(id); if not x: raise NotFoundError(...)`, check
`app/utils/validators.py` (the existing home for cross-service helpers like
`validate_uuid`) for one first, and add it there if it doesn't exist yet
rather than writing a sixth copy.

## Batch a query, don't run one per loop iteration

Good existing pattern — `app/services/viagens_service.py`, in
`_popular_dados_da_viagem` (explicitly commented "Batch load ... to avoid N+1
queries"): fetch once, index into a dict, then loop over the dict:

```python
alunos = db.session.query(Aluno).filter(Aluno.usuario_id.in_(aluno_ids)).all()
alunos_map = {a.usuario_id: a for a in alunos}
for inscricao in inscricoes:
    aluno = alunos_map.get(inscricao.aluno_id)
```

The same file does not apply its own lesson consistently: `gerar_viagens_em_lote`
has a query issued inside a nested `for rota in rotas: for horario in
horarios_validos: db.session.query(Viagem.id)...` loop — N+1 against the
database, worst at exactly the scale (`stress`, `breakpoint`) the
load-testing harness models. When you're about to write a query inside a
`for`, stop and fetch the collection you need in one query before the loop
instead — the pattern to copy is one function above in the same file.
